    "chapter_name": "Motion In Two Dimensions",
    "year": "2023",
    "shift": "unknown_shift"
  },
  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A slide with a frictionless curved surface, which becomes horizontal at its lower end, is fixed on the terrace of a building of height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mi>h</mi></math> from the ground, as shown in the figure. A spherical ball of mass <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math>&nbsp;is released on the slide from rest at a height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>h</mi></math>&nbsp;from the top of the terrace. The ball leaves the slide with a velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;and falls on the ground at a distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math>&nbsp;from the building making an angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi></math>&nbsp;with the horizontal. It bounces off with a velocity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>v</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover></math>&nbsp;and reaches a maximum height <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></math>. The acceleration due to gravity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>g</mi></math>&nbsp;and the coefficient of restitution of the ground is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></mfrac></math>. Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0003_f9438bdee105.png\" style=\"width: 500px; height: 282px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mover><mi>u</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt><mover><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>v</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt><mfenced><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover><mo>-</mo><mover><mi>z</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover></mrow></mfenced></math>",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msup></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>",
      "is_correct": true
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "A",
    "C",
    "D"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p><img src=\"assets\\st0003_798b75d30326.png\" style=\"width: 500px; height: 312px;\" /></p><p>Using energy conservation,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>m</mi><msup><mfenced><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;is the horizontal component of the velocity throughout the flight. For vertical component of the velocity(just before collision with the ground), we can use equation of motion</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mfenced><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>z</mi></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow></mfenced><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>&#8658;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>6</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt></math></p><p>Angle&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>&#952;</mi></math>&nbsp;can be written as,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>tan</mi><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mi>u</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msup></math></p><p>Horizontal distance covered just before the collision will be,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mi>T</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>g</mi></mfrac></mfenced></msqrt><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt><msqrt><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>h</mi></mrow><mi>g</mi></mfrac></mfenced></msqrt><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt><mi>h</mi></math></p><p>After collision, only velocity along <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>z</mi></math>-direction changes. Therefore,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>e</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>z</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt></math>&nbsp;and hence</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mover><mi>v</mi><mo>&#8594;</mo></mover><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mover><mi>i</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mover><mi>k</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover></math></p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mover><mi>i</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mi>k</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover></mrow></mfenced><mspace linebreak=\"newline\"></mspace><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover><mo>+</mo><mover><mi>z</mi><mo>&#8743;</mo></mover></mrow></mfenced></math></p><p>Now, maximum height achieved after collision will be,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mfrac><msup><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mn>2</mn></msup><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mi>h</mi></math>.</p><p>Finally,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>u</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msqrt><mn>2</mn><mi>g</mi><mi>h</mi></msqrt><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>&#952;</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>60</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi></msup><mo>,</mo><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mi>d</mi><msub><mi>h</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msqrt><mn>3</mn></msqrt></math>.</p>"
  }
}